text:
The diff changes the unit alternation in `parse` from `years?|yrs?|y` to `years?|yrs|y`. The only practical effect is that the singular abbreviation `yr` (as in `'1yr'`) no longer matches the regex, since `yrs` requires the trailing `s` and `y` alone can't consume the `r` before the `$` anchor.

In the original, `ms('1yr')` matches with `n = 1` and `type = 'yr'`, returning `1 * y = 31557600000`. After the change, the regex fails to match, so `parse` returns `undefined`, and the exported function silently returns `undefined` (the `throw` only covers non-string/non-number inputs). That's an observable behaviour change for any caller using the `yr` spelling — e.g. `ms('1yr')`, `ms('-2yr')`, `ms('1.5yr')`.

The library's own suite doesn't exercise the bare `yr` spelling, which is why it still passes. Note that `ms('yr')` without a number never parsed (the number group requires at least one digit), so `'1yr'`-style input is the right probe.

```json
{"defect": true, "testFile": {"path": "proof-test.js", "content": "var assert = require('assert');
var ms = require('./');

describe('proof', function () {
  it('fails on the patched build', function () {
    // The singular unit "yr" must still parse (1 year = 365.25 days in ms)
    assert.strictEqual(ms('1yr'), 31557600000);
    assert.strictEqual(ms('-2yr'), -63115200000);
  });
});
"}, "note": "Changing `yrs?` to `yrs` in the parse regex drops support for the singular unit 'yr', so ms('1yr') now returns undefined instead of 31557600000. This is observable to callers and not covered by the library's own tests."}
```

